  "response": "I’m using the `crimes-codebase-risk` review workflow to turn the scan into merge decisions. I’ll separate true merge risks from likely test/document false positives instead of treating all 55 findings as equivalent.\n\nThe repository-local skill path isn’t under this fixture runner; I’m resolving it from the workspace-level location listed for this session, then I’ll apply its review guidance.\n\n## Verdict: block merge\n\nThe five requested families contain 36 findings: 2 blockers, 4 warnings, and 30 ignores. Because this PR starts from an empty baseline, both new high-severity large files are blockers even though their `agent_risk` is only `0.30`; the score prioritizes review but does not cancel high severity.\n\n### Block merge\n\n| Finding | Severity / agent risk | Decision and justification |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `crime_00004` — `lib/command.js`, 2,179 lines | high / `0.30` | **Block.** At 7.3× the threshold, this central implementation creates a substantial context and coupling hazard. Split by command parsing, subcommand execution, configuration, and help concerns. |\n| `crime_00005` — `typings/index.d.ts`, 891 lines | high / `0.30` | **Block.** This is 3× the threshold and defines the public type contract. Its `test_gap: 0.75` makes accidental API drift especially concerning. Split into coherent declaration modules while preserving the public entry point. |\n\n### Warn, but do not block\n\n| Finding | Severity / agent risk | Decision and justification |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `crime_00007` — `typings/index.test-d.ts`, 480 lines | medium / `0.30` | **Warn.** A 1.6× oversized type-test file is harder to navigate, but it is test code rather than runtime logic. Split by API area. |\n| `crime_00035` — weak `exitOverride()` test | low / `0.23` | **Warn.** Checking `_exitCallback` only for truthiness does not prove it is callable or that the intended callback was copied. Assert its type, identity, or observable behavior. |\n| `crime_00043` — `lib/help.js`,